- Retrieved practices:
- Container index and iterator bounds checking
WHAT: Accessing containers or arrays using indices or iterators that are outside the valid range (for example, pos >= tableSize) causes out-of-bounds reads/writes.
WHY: Out-of-bounds access can corrupt memory, trigger undefined behavior, and lead to exploitable vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Always validate indices and iterator positions against container size before access; prefer APIs or templates that deduce array bounds at compile time and centralize bounds checks in a single, reused function.
EXAMPLE:
Before (unsafe): `table[pos] = value;`
After (safe): `if (pos >= tableSize) { /* handle error */ return; } table[pos] = value;`

- Avoid information leakage when passing C++ class objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing raw C++ class objects (by value or raw memory copy) across a trust boundary can leak sensitive data via padding bits and unused bit-fields whose contents are implementation-defined.
WHY: Attackers receiving such objects may read uninitialized padding/bit-field data, exposing prior in-memory values (keys, pointers, secrets) and enabling information disclosure or further exploitation.
HOW: Do not transmit raw class layouts; instead, define explicit serialized representations (e.g., structs without padding, versioned wire formats) and copy only initialized fields into these representations before sending across trust boundaries.
EXAMPLE: Before: `send(sock, &obj, sizeof(obj), 0);` After: define a packed/serialized DTO with only needed fields, populate it from `obj`, then `send(sock, &dto, sizeof(dto), 0);`

- Do not compare or access padding bits in C++ object representations
WHAT: Accessing or comparing the raw object representation (for example, via std::memcmp on whole objects) reads padding bytes that are not part of the object’s value representation, leading to incorrect equality checks or logic decisions.
WHY: Padding bytes are implementation-defined and may contain indeterminate values, so relying on them can cause inconsistent behavior, subtle bugs, and undefined behavior across builds or platforms.
HOW: Compare only the logical value fields of an object (for example, by defining and using operator== or dedicated comparison functions) and avoid using raw memory functions (memcmp, memcmp-like APIs) on entire class/struct objects that may contain padding.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant: `if (!std::memcmp(&s1, &s2, sizeof(S))) { ... }` where `S` is a struct with padding; Compliant: define `bool operator==(const S& lhs, const S& rhs)` that compares each data member and use `if (s1 == s2) { ... }`.

- Prefer C++ member functions and operators over std::memcmp for object comparison
WHAT: Using std::memcmp() (or similar raw memory functions) to compare non–standard-layout C++ objects (especially with virtual functions/vtables) compares object representations, including vtable pointers, and can yield incorrect equality results.
WHY: Incorrect comparisons can cause logic errors, bypass or trigger security checks incorrectly, and violate rules about accessing bits not in the object’s value representation.
HOW: Implement and use type-safe comparison via overloaded operators (e.g., operator==) or dedicated member functions; avoid raw memory comparison for objects with virtual functions, padding, or non-trivial members.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `if (!std::memcmp(&c1, &c2, sizeof(C))) { ... }`
After (safe): `if (c1 == c2) { ... }` with `bool C::operator==(const C& other) const { return i == other.i; }`

- EXP53-CPP: Do not read uninitialized memory
WHAT: Reading the value of an object with automatic or dynamic storage duration before it is explicitly initialized (except for allowed unsigned narrow character cases) causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to crashes, data corruption, security vulnerabilities, and unreliable behavior across compilers and optimization levels.
HOW: Explicitly initialize all local and dynamically allocated objects before first read; do not rely on uninitialized data for entropy; prefer zero-initializing allocations (e.g., std::calloc) or manually setting values after std::malloc/new; ensure realloc-extended regions are initialized before use.
EXAMPLE:
Noncompliant: `int i; std::cout << i;`
Compliant: `int i = 0; std::cout << i;`

- Avoid information leakage when passing objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Failing to fully initialize all fields (including padding and reserved fields) of objects or structs before copying them across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized memory contents.
WHY: Attackers can read sensitive kernel or process memory, potentially exposing secrets, internal state, or enabling further exploitation.
HOW: Before returning or sending any struct/class instance to less-trusted code, explicitly initialize or zero all fields (including padding/reserved areas), or use APIs/types that guarantee zero-initialization.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `MyStruct s; some_fields_set(s); send_to_user(s);` After: `MyStruct s{}; some_fields_set(s); secure_memset_unused_fields(s); send_to_user(s);`



